  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=2?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}, & x\\ne 2 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-12, & x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-2)}{(x-4)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-2)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2}{x-4}=\\frac{2+2}{2-4}=-2.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x) \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-12 & =-2 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-10 & =0 \\\\ (k+2)(k-5) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=2,$ we need $k=-2$ or $k=5.$",
